Title: "Declares"
Id: "94516121-2dd0-4ec4-9a4e-b50a4ae45753"

Text: "`Declares:` introduces a reusable type or interface: a shape and the facts
that every instance carries. It does not supply a concrete value.

```mlg-fragment
[CommandHeader]
Declares: <FormOrDeclaration>
using?: <DeclarationStatement>+
when?: <ClauseUnion>+
extends?: <ExtendsItem>+
specifies?: <IsOrViaItem>+
satisfies?: <ClauseUnion>+
Requires?: <RequiresItemUnion>+
Enables?: <EnablesItemUnion>+
Documented?: <DocumentedItemUnion>+
Justification?: <HaveGroup>+
Aliases?: <AliasItemUnion>+
Writing?: \"<WritingAlias>\"+
References?: <ResourceHeader>+
Metadata?: <MetadataItemUnion>+
Id?: <OpenText>
```

The target may name the type it extends directly:

```mlg-fragment
[\magma]
Declares: M ::= (X, *) is \set via X
specifies: * is \binary.operation:on{X}
Documented:
. called: \"magma\"
```

The equivalent `extends:` section is useful when one target extends several
types through different views. A group may use the relation on the `Declares:`
target or `extends:`, but not both.

```mlg-fragment
[\foo]
Declares: F ::= (X, *, +, 0, 1)
extends:
. F is \bar via (X, +, 0)
. F is \baz via (X, *, 1)
```

`specifies:` gives type, specification, or assignment-backed facts for the
target's components. `satisfies:` gives proposition clauses that every instance
must satisfy. Facts are processed in order. A `:=` assignment may bind a part
to an already-known value and participates in later capability reduction.

The heading is required. Header parameters normally need facts in `when:`.
`Documented:` must contain `called:` or `written:`. Only `Declares:` may use a
spec-infix heading such as `[A \:subset:/ B]`."
Id: "bf47cb67-b5e3-44de-add0-6b767a92aec8"
